Z||000005||Fiasco||04-14-2000||08:24 PM||||Why not ATF?<BR>I still just use plain ol' DOT 3, but I thought some others here had switched to ATF and weren't having any probs. Just curious.<P>And I'd have to go with an Old Style.<BR>-I know, I know, nasty stuff... but here in Wisconsin we get deported if we don't drink the stuff.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiasco (edited 04-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||04-14-2000||09:21 PM||||Stop!  Don't put ATF in your car; it will blow out the seals.  Almost everyone who used this in the past has changed it out, along with their slave and master cylinders.||||reg||11
Z||000007||Fiasco||04-14-2000||10:33 PM||||OK OK I wasn't gonna anyway... DOT 3 works fine for me.  I was just curious as to what probs it would cause.<P>Thanks for the info.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000008||Fiasco||04-14-2000||10:49 PM||||1FST2M6-<BR>Do you have to bleed the system very often this way? Clutch disengage right at the floor?  Just curious, 'cause I can't see how you're getting all the air out without bleeding the slave cylinder, too.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||04-15-2000||06:02 AM||||<BR>I want to make it absolutely clear that I am NOT promoting the use of ATF in the clutch hydraulic system. However, I have been VERY satisfied with my clutch since introducing ATF into my hydraulic system. It's been eight months now and the clutch still feels brand new. I don't have to bleed it anymore and it disengages properly all the time.<P>Having read the horror tales here from some unfortunate people, I'd only advise someone try ATF as a last resort BEFORE they change their slave and/or master cylinders. It appears that using ATF with NEW hardware is a disaster, as the seals swell up so much that the pistons won't move. <P>For me it was a gamble that paid off.||||reg||1
